{
  "requirement": "An application supporting tracked source inspection returns `Self::NodeData` for the requested path and selector. The SDK interns that data in the domain's cache to obtain `NodeId<Self>`. An unsupported or invalid address returns an error.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Domain: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n    fn source_node(\n        &self,\n        item: &OutlineData,\n    ) -> Result<Self::NodeData, SourceError> { ... }\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations derived from `requirements.sdk.sources.source_nodes`:**\n1. For a supported path and selector, `Domain::source_node` returns `Self::NodeData`.\n2. The SDK interns that data in the domain cache to obtain a `NodeId<Self>`.\n3. An unsupported or invalid address returns an error.\n4. The trait signature matches the one shown.\n\n**How the tests cover them:**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/project_sources.rs#mod[tests]/fn[direct_source_reads_preserve_mapping_errors_and_record_only_successful_returns]` is the main test. Its test domain's `source_node` rejects an unknown path (`unknown.txt`) and a bad selector (`line[unknown]` on a known path).\n  - For each rejected address, it asserts that `read_source` returns an error containing \"unsupported source item\". It also asserts that no dependency is recorded and no domain read happens. This covers the unsupported/invalid-address obligation for both the path and the selector.\n  - For the supported address (`source.txt` with an empty selector), the test domain returns `\"source-node\"` data. The test asserts that the recorded dependency is `domain.cache().intern_node(\"source-node\")` and that the domain read hit `source-node`. This shows the SDK interned the returned data into a `NodeId` and used it.\n  - The successful read returns the expected content and reads run twice in total. The saved record is current, and it becomes stale after the content changes.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configured_agent_uses_domain_source_tools_in_independent_executions]` covers the other call sites end to end.\n  - Its `TestDomain::source_node` maps `visible/selected.txt`, `alpha.txt`, `beta.txt` and `unused.txt` to distinct `TestNode` ids. The test asserts that each returned id is interned, and that the dependency list holds exactly those node ids with their content fingerprints. It runs twice.\n  - It also asserts the `OutlineData` addresses the domain received, which shows the requested path and selector reach `source_node`.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks the published API inventory. I treat it as covering the trait-signature obligation only loosely, and that is acceptable here.\n- The default trait behavior, an `Err(Invalid)` for a domain with no mapping, is asserted in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[domain_relationships_accept_opaque_types_and_send_only_errors]`. That test carries no `{test}` reference to the subject. The requirement doesn't state the default, so I don't count this as a gap. It supports the \"unsupported returns an error\" obligation.\n- The search-hit mapping failure is tested in `an_unmapped_hit_fails_the_search`. It is marked for `requirements.sdk.graph.search.domain_node`, a separate requirement, so no reference to the subject is needed.\n\n**Gaps:** I found none. Every obligation has an exercising test with assertions on the outcome, and the error and success paths are both asserted. No acknowledgments (`{fixme}`) were present or needed.",
  "references": "f2a80deb786be0869fd8f9959f206506d5e92b0c12b7cd54d96c008cea83d3f0",
